{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The compact route is to prove all four propositions independently, then assemble each `Iff` from two true statements and finally form the conjunction.

1. Prove the smooth-number approximation as a convergence statement
   \[
   \sum_{m\in \mathrm{smoothNumbers}(N)} f(m)\longrightarrow \sum_m f(m).
   \]
   The sets of \(N\)-smooth numbers are increasing and eventually contain every positive integer; indeed \(0<m<N\) implies \(m\) is \(N\)-smooth. Unconditional summability gives uniform control of subseries supported outside a sufficiently large finite set, so the complementary subseries tends to zero. The assumption \(f(0)=0\) is essential because \(0\) is never smooth; without it the restricted sums would converge to the total sum minus \(f(0)\). See the characterization of [`Nat.smoothNumbers`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/SmoothNumbers.html).

2. Prove the `primesBelow` recurrence by membership extensionality:
   \[
   p<n+1 \iff p<n\ \lor\ p=n.
   \]
   Thus the new element is exactly \(n\) when \(n\) is prime; otherwise the finset is unchanged.

3. For the first analytic identity, use directly that `ZMod 1` is a singleton and the modulus-one specialization
   `ZMod.completedLFunction_modOne_eq`. It states precisely that every \(\Phi : \mathrm{ZMod}\,1\to\mathbb C\) gives \(\Phi(1)\) times the completed Riemann zeta function. [Mathlib documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/ZMod.html).

4. For the second identity, use `ArithmeticFunction.LSeries_zeta_eq`. Conceptually, arithmetic \(\zeta(n)\) is \(1\) for \(n>0\) and \(0\) at \(0\), while the zeroth `LSeries` term is always defined to be zero, so its L-series equals that of the constant sequence \(1\). [Mathlib documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/Dirichlet.html#ArithmeticFunction.LSeries_zeta_eq).

With these four intermediate results, both equivalences are immediate from truth of their two sides."}}
